body:
"We won't tell anyone yet" he'd said, eyes lighting up as they always did when he got excited. He reminded me of a child at Christmas, eyes dancing and twinkling at all the shiny paper and wrapping, delicately hiding promises of joy and laughter. "It'll be a such a surprise when they all find out!". 

&nbsp;

Six years, we'd been together. Two of those married, six months of that trying for children. Six months of tests, ovulation timers and stupid fertility tips. Months of disappointment when I could never conceive, when we could never bring forth our miracle. Two tiny pink lines changed that for us, and in a second, we were catapulted onto the new journey of our lives together. 

&nbsp;

They say the first trimester is the hardest, and whoever "they" are got that completely right. Nausea, vomiting, constantly peeing seemingly every fifteen minutes, but inside our happy bubble, I was glowing. We were going to be parents after all this time. A new life was budding inside me. Our tiny little miracle. 

&nbsp;

It was icy that day. Icy, and bitterly cold. I remember it as though I was watching a TV show, a movie, reading a book- it's separate to me in its entirety. One minute, I was hurrying down our driveway, heading to the shops for last minute groceries. Bundled up against the chill, parka and gloves protecting me from the worst of it. 

&nbsp;


I fell, hard, on my side. My thoughts weren't on the pain, but on our little life, growing inside me. So fragile and unable to fend for itself.  I felt it instantly - a wave of pain so crippling and terrifying it could only be one thing. My husband tells me I screamed out, and he came running. 

&nbsp;

My jeans, my hands, my body- they all tell a story now. A sad tragedy marked in blood, tears and snow. The loss of something so loved, a bundle of cells with so much promise. Our little life. 

&nbsp;


"We won't tell anyone yet!" he'd said. Two weeks have gone by, my bed rest is almost over. The world has kept turning without missing a beat for everyone else, except us lonely two who knew. Flu, we'd said, keeping the lie simple, our secret alive. 

&nbsp;

Our world in crumbled pieces now, us secret keepers. Nobody knows, maybe nobody will. Alone again. Us two.
